<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hormizd IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/590">590</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Hormozd IV</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hormizd IV was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> from 579 to 590. Son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a>, he waged wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the Turks, and around 580 he abolished the monarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, governing it through a Persian official. His conflict with the nobility and with his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-chobin">Bahram Chobin</a> led to his deposition and murder in 590, after which his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> took the throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hormizd-iv.jpg" alt="Hormizd IV" width="255" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drachma_of_Hormidz_IV_-_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hormizd IV was the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a>, and his mother is said to have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> or Turkic princess, though sources differ on her origin and a modern historian has suggested a Hephthalite one. Historians consider it more plausible that he was born around 540. Khosrow I, aware that he had shown himself a capable leader, appointed him heir, and he came to the throne in 579. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> calls him a Turkzad, or son of a Turk, but the Iranologist Shapur Shahbazi rejects this as a chronological difficulty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Hormizd continued his father's policies. He supported the landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>, the dehqans, against the aristocracy, protected the lower classes and thwarted the Zoroastrian priesthood. He resorted to executions to do so, killing many priests, including the chief priest, and thousands of nobles, among them the minister Bozorgmehr. When the priesthood asked him to persecute Christians, he refused, wishing that all his subjects might exercise their religion freely. Persian sources are therefore hostile to him, while modern historians tend to see a well-meaning ruler who was overambitious.</p><p>He inherited a war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. When peace talks began, he demanded the yearly tribute paid under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>, and they broke down. Fighting continued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and Byzantine forces under Maurice advanced towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> in 581 before retreating. About 580, after the death of the Iberian client king Bakur III, who left two underage sons, Hormizd abolished the Chosroid monarchy and, by winning the Iberian aristocracy, incorporated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> into the empire. In 588 the Turkic khagan Bagha Qaghan invaded south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a>. The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-chobin">Bahram Chobin</a> defeated him at the Battle of Hyrcanian Rock in April 588 and killed him with an arrow in 589.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jealous of Bahram, Hormizd had him disgraced and dismissed and sent him a chain and a spindle. Bahram rebelled, which began the Sasanian civil war of 589-591, and the army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> went over to him. Hormizd's brothers-in-law Vistahm and Vinduyih overthrew him in a seemingly bloodless palace revolution, had him blinded and placed his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> on the throne. In June 590 they strangled Hormizd with his own turban. Whether Khosrow II was involved is disputed; Theophylact Simocatta says so, the Muslim historian Dinawari does not, and Khosrow later had both his uncles executed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hormizd IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When King Bakur III died around 580, the Persian king <b>Hormizd IV</b> abolished the monarchy and governed Iberia through a marzban, while the great nobles kept their estates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.09,44.58&amp;year=580" aria-label="Ctesiphon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5691 962 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hormizd IV"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6141 1232h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6141 1232h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.09,44.58&amp;year=580">What was here in 580 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
